INVENTED DATA. None of this came from imgur.

imgur_battle.py read a user's comments from

    http://imgur.com/user/<name>/index/newest/page/<N>/hit.json?scrolling

paging until a response came back empty. That endpoint is gone and nothing
archived it for arbitrary users, so there is no real data to replay. These four
responses exist for one reason: to make the scoring bug in battle() happen on
purpose, with the smallest input that shows it.

battle() keeps one high_score across the RUNNING totals, updated after every
post, and then declares as winners the users whose FINAL total equals it. imgur
comment points can be negative, so a running total can peak above where it ends:

    post        alice  bob    running high
    Xq7Lm2a      +10    +1        10
    Pz4Kd8w       -6    +2        10      (alice ends on 4, bob on 3)

Nobody finishes on 10, so the winner list is empty and the program prints
"The winners are []!". Dockerfile.web asserts that line.

The order matters, and it is Python 2's set iteration order over the shared
post hashes, which is deterministic for a given interpreter; Xq7Lm2a comes out
first on 2.7.18. The comments that only one user made (Hn2Rt5c, Wd9Fg1s) are
there so get_shared() has something to exclude.

example_alice and example_bob are placeholders, not anyone's imgur account.
